##############################################################################
#  GENERATED from the anomaly playbook definitions. Do not hand edit.
#
#  This is the agent's ENTRY POINT. The anomaly report is a set of events,
#  each emitted by a playbook that matched a pattern. Unlike counters, events
#  are symbols: discrete, already interpreted, scoped to a device and a time,
#  and drawn from a finite enumerable vocabulary. That is what makes them
#  usable as the thing hypotheses are generated from.
#
#  Each event maps onto the symptom wording the causal model uses. Keeping
#  the mapping here rather than in fault-model.n4l is the point of the split:
#  playbooks change often, knowledge about how fabrics fail does not. A new
#  playbook adds a mapping and touches nothing else.
##############################################################################

-Anomaly playbook event vocabulary

 :: playbooks, event vocabulary, agent input ::

 playbook: interface congestion (doc-src) source: anomaly playbook definitions
        "  (emits) event: sustained egress queue drops on a fabric port
        "  (emits) event: interface utilisation imbalance within a link group

 playbook: adjacency stability (emits) event: routing adjacency reset
        "  (emits) event: repeated neighbour state transitions on one interface

 playbook: reachability probe (emits) event: inter rack probe loss above threshold
        "  (emits) event: probe loss affecting a subset of destination prefixes

 playbook: optics health (emits) event: transceiver receive power below threshold

 playbook: resource utilisation (emits) event: forwarding table utilisation above threshold

 playbook: config compliance (emits) event: running configuration diverged from template

 playbook: flow completion (emits) event: large transfer completion time regression
        "  (emits) event: transfer stalls after session establishment

 :: event to symptom mapping ::

 # The join between the machine feed and the causal model. An event that
 # maps to no symptom is an event the model cannot reason about, and a
 # symptom with no event is a blind spot. See queries/blind-spots.sql.

 event: sustained egress queue drops on a fabric port (describe) symptom: intermittent loss with no error counters incrementing
        "                                             (describe) symptom: loss correlates with time of day or with a batch job

 event: interface utilisation imbalance within a link group (describe) symptom: one uplink congested while its peers are idle
        "                                                   (describe) symptom: loss that appears only above a traffic threshold

 event: routing adjacency reset (describe) symptom: protocol adjacency flaps without a clean reason
 event: repeated neighbour state transitions on one interface (describe) symptom: protocol adjacency flaps without a clean reason

 event: inter rack probe loss above threshold (describe) symptom: connectivity works within a rack and fails between racks
        "                                     (describe) symptom: intermittent loss with no error counters incrementing

 event: probe loss affecting a subset of destination prefixes (describe) symptom: newly added prefixes or hosts are unreachable while existing ones are fine

 event: transceiver receive power below threshold (describe) symptom: traffic blackholes in one direction only

 event: forwarding table utilisation above threshold (describe) symptom: newly added prefixes or hosts are unreachable while existing ones are fine
        "                                            (describe) symptom: the problem appeared after a scale event rather than after a change

 event: running configuration diverged from template (describe) symptom: behaviour changed with no corresponding change record

 event: large transfer completion time regression (describe) symptom: small flows work and large transfers stall
 event: transfer stalls after session establishment (describe) symptom: tcp session establishes then hangs

 :: playbook coverage, known gaps ::

 playbook library (note) Coverage is a property of the playbook set, not of the causal model. A fault whose every symptom lacks an emitting playbook is invisible to the agent no matter how good its reasoning, and the agent should say so rather than silently omitting it
        "         (question) no playbook currently emits an event for asymmetric neighbour discovery, which is the primary tell for cabling and patch errors
        "         (tbd) a neighbour consistency playbook comparing discovered adjacencies against the cabling plan would close the largest gap in this library

 :: playbooks, what each already detects and remediates ::

 # The negative evidence map. A playbook exists because the problem was
 # already characterisable by counters AND already has remediation, so a
 # fault listed here is a SOLVED problem. When a complaint arrives that no
 # playbook explained, these are the faults to look away from, and the ones
 # absent from this list are the ones to look at.

 playbook: interface congestion (detects) fault: buffer exhaustion from microbursts
        "  (detects) fault: ecmp hash polarisation
        "  (threshold) threshold: queue drop rate above a sustained rate over five minutes

 playbook: adjacency stability (detects) fault: control plane policer discarding protocol traffic
        "  (threshold) threshold: two or more adjacency resets within an hour

 playbook: optics health (detects) fault: unidirectional link fault
        "  (threshold) threshold: receive power below the transceiver specified minimum

 playbook: resource utilisation (detects) fault: hardware table exhaustion
        "  (threshold) threshold: utilisation above ninety percent of the profile capacity

 playbook: config compliance (detects) fault: configuration drift from template
        "  (threshold) threshold: any diff against the rendered template

 playbook: reachability probe (detects) fault: overlay to underlay mapping inconsistency
        "  (threshold) threshold: probe loss above one percent between rack pairs

 # Deliberately absent, and this is the point of the layer:
 #   fault: mtu mismatch on a transit hop     -- no playbook characterises it
 #   fault: cabling or patch error            -- no playbook characterises it
 # Both are therefore prime suspects whenever a complaint arrives that the
 # playbook library did not explain.

 threshold: queue drop rate above a sustained rate over five minutes (note) A user notices a stall lasting two seconds. This threshold needs five minutes. The band between what a person perceives and what a playbook fires on is where complaints live, and it is why a silent playbook demotes a hypothesis rather than eliminating it
